course: history
lesson: 20
title: The Middle Ages begin: the Vikings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The Middle Ages are the thousand years in Europe between the fall of Rome, around 500, and around 1500. People later called them a gap in the middle, but they built cathedrals, universities and new inventions, so they were not just dark and stupid.
explain: The Vikings were Norse people from Denmark, Norway and Sweden. Going viking meant going on a sea raid, but most Norse people were farmers, fishers and traders, and they never wore horned helmets; that came from opera costumes in the 1800s.
explain: Their longships were light and fast and could sail up shallow rivers, so Vikings could appear far from home without warning, like a speedboat that also fits in a stream. From 793 they raided, traded and settled across Europe.
explain: They settled in England, Ireland and Normandy, founded Dublin, reached Iceland, Greenland and even North America, and in the east they travelled the rivers to Constantinople and helped found Kyivan Rus. By the 1000s their lands were Christian kingdoms, and the Viking Age faded.
fact: The Middle Ages are the thousand years in Europe between the fall of Rome, around 500, and around 1500, and they built cathedrals, universities and new inventions.
fact: Vikings were Norse people from Denmark, Norway and Sweden; viking really meant going on a sea raid, and most Norse people were farmers, fishers and traders.
fact: Vikings did not wear horned helmets; that idea came from costumes in a 19th-century opera.
fact: Viking longships were light and fast, with sails and oars, and could cross the sea and sail up shallow rivers.
fact: In 793 Vikings raided the monastery of Lindisfarne in England, and this is often called the start of the Viking Age.
fact: Vikings settled in England, Ireland, Scotland and Normandy in France, and founded Dublin as a town.
fact: Many English words come from Norse, like sky, egg, knife and Thursday, which means Thor's day.
fact: Vikings settled Iceland and Greenland, and Iceland's Althing, founded in 930, is one of the oldest parliaments still working.
fact: Around the year 1000 Leif Erikson sailed to North America, which the Norse called Vinland, and a Viking site has been found in Newfoundland, Canada, but the Norse did not stay there for long.
fact: Norse traders from Sweden travelled down the rivers of eastern Europe, traded with Constantinople and Baghdad, and took part in founding the state of Kyivan Rus; historians still argue about how big their part was.
fact: Vikings believed in Odin, Thor with his hammer, Loki the trickster, and Valhalla, the hall of fallen warriors; Marvel's Thor and Loki are very different from the old myths.
fact: Vikings wrote with runes, letters of straight lines made for cutting into wood and stone, and runes were mostly writing, not magic spells.
fact: By the 1000s the Norse lands had become Christian kingdoms and raids faded; in 1066 a Norwegian king was defeated in England, and the Normans, descendants of Vikings in France, conquered England the same year.
exercise: Which years do the Middle Ages roughly cover? | From about 500 to about 1500 | From about 1500 to about 2000 | From about 3000 BC to 500 BC | From 1066 to 1100
exercise: From which countries did the Vikings come? | Denmark, Norway and Sweden | Italy, Greece and the south of Spain | Egypt and Sudan | China and Japan
exercise: What did going viking mean? | Going on a sea raid | Going to church | Going fishing at home | Going to school
exercise: What did most Norse people do for a living? | Farming, fishing and trading | Raiding monasteries every single day of the year | Building pyramids | Working in factories
exercise: Where did the idea of horned helmets come from? | Opera costumes in the 1800s | Real Viking graves dug up in Norway | Roman paintings | Viking rune stones
exercise: Why could longships surprise people far inland? | They could sail up shallow rivers | They could fly over hills | They had wheels for the roads | They sailed under the ground through tunnels
exercise: Which monastery did Vikings raid in 793? | Lindisfarne | Hagia Sophia | Knossos | Machu Picchu
exercise: Which city did Vikings found in Ireland? | Dublin | London in England | Paris in France | Rome in Italy
exercise: Which English word comes from Norse? | Sky | Table | Pizza | Chocolate
exercise: What does Thursday mean? | Thor's day | Third day | Thunder day of Rome | Thirsty day
exercise: What is Iceland's Althing? | One of the oldest parliaments still working | A Viking longship kept in a museum | A rune stone | A volcano
exercise: Who sailed to North America around the year 1000? | Leif Erikson | Christopher Columbus | Marco Polo | Julius Caesar
exercise: Where has a Viking site been found in North America? | Newfoundland, Canada | Florida, USA | Mexico City | Brazil
exercise: Which state did Norse traders take part in founding in the east? | Kyivan Rus | The Byzantine Empire | The Inca Empire | Ancient Persia
exercise: What was Thor's weapon? | A hammer | A bow and arrows | A trident | A spear of light
exercise: What were runes? | Letters made of straight lines for cutting in wood and stone | Magic spells only, never ordinary words | Viking coins | Pictures of gods painted on shields and on the walls of halls
exercise: Who conquered England in 1066? | The Normans, descendants of Vikings | The Romans | The Aztecs | The Mongols
test: cartoon vikings all have horns on their helmets. right? | No, that came from opera costumes in the 1800s | Yes, every Viking wore them, even in battle | Yes, but only on Sundays | No, they wore crowns
test: Bro says the Middle Ages were dark and stupid, nothing happened. Fair? | No, they built cathedrals, universities and inventions | Yes, nobody built anything for a thousand years | Yes, people forgot how to talk | No, because it was the Stone Age
test: Marvel's Thor is a superhero with a big smile. What was Thor for the Vikings? | A god of the old myths, quite different from the films | A real Norse king who ruled Denmark | A Roman soldier | A Greek hero
test: If Vikings reached America about 1000, why do people say Columbus "found" it? | The Vikings did not stay, and Native peoples were already there | The Vikings never went at all | Columbus was a Viking | America was empty
test: You say "egg", "knife" and "sky" at breakfast. Who gave English those words? | The Norse | The Romans | The Greeks | The Aztecs
test: Europa Universalis 5 starts in 1337. Why are there no Viking raids in it? | The Viking Age had ended in the 1000s | Vikings were too far away to fit on the map | The game forgot them | Vikings only started in 1500
test: What would happen if a longship were heavy and deep? | It could not sail up shallow rivers | It would fly faster than any other ship afloat | It would never sink, even in storms | It would carry no one
test: Someone says runes were only for magic spells. What is closer to the truth? | Runes were mostly ordinary writing | Runes were never used for anything | Runes were Roman numbers | Runes were Chinese letters
test: Why is Lindisfarne in 793 remembered? | It is often called the start of the Viking Age | It was the end of the Roman Empire in the west | Columbus landed there | The Normans were born there
test: oi how did vikings get all the way to constantinople, there's no sea from sweden? | They travelled down the rivers of eastern Europe | They walked over the Alps | They flew | They sailed all the way round Africa to get there
test: A Norwegian king lost in England in 1066, then the Normans won. Who were the Normans? | Descendants of Vikings in France | Roman soldiers | Greek sailors | Inuit hunters
exam: Identify the approximate chronological limits of the period designated the Middle Ages in Europe. | From approximately 500 to approximately 1500 | From approximately 1500 to approximately 1900 | From approximately 3000 BC to the foundation of Rome | From 1066 to 1100
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning horned helmets attributed to the Vikings. | They derive from operatic costume of the nineteenth century | They are attested in numerous Viking burials | They were mandated by Norse law | They were worn solely by kings
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the occupation of the greater number of Norse people? | Agriculture, fishing and commerce | Continual raiding by sea | Service in the Roman legions as paid soldiers abroad | The construction of pyramids
exam: Ascertain the person who, circa the year 1000, reached the coast of North America, designated by the Norse as Vinland. | Leif Erikson | Christopher Columbus | Harald Hardrada | Marco Polo
